INT. COVERED BRIDGE - STALLED STAGECOACH - DAY

Timbers creak overhead. A river roars beneath. The coach sits dead centre, one wheel jammed in a gap in the planking, horses stamping and blowing at the near end.

SHERIFF AMOS TULL, fifties, a deep-lined face under a flat hat, stands at the coach door with a shotgun cradled in the crook of his arm. Across the bridge from him, CORDELIA RUSK, thirties, in a black duster, waits on a grey mare with a rifle across her pommel. Between them, in the coach window, DEPUTY WENDELL AUBREY, twenty, sweats through his collar. Beside the wheel, REVEREND IGNATIUS MBEKI, sixty, kneels in the dust with an open Bible, whispering.

TULL
(to everyone)
Nobody move.

CORDELIA
Nobody's moving, Sheriff.

TULL
Then why's your hand on that stock?

CORDELIA
Habit.

TULL
(to Wendell)
Get that wheel loose.

WENDELL
I'm trying, sir.

TULL
Try harder.

Wendell climbs down. He crouches by the wheel and shoves at it with both hands. The coach does not budge. The river roars. Cordelia nudges the mare one step forward.

MBEKI
(murmuring)
Lord, if it be Thy will.

CORDELIA
Preacher, I'd say it's the Lord's will you stay put.

MBEKI
Madam, I am praying for your soul.

CORDELIA
Then pray for the wheel.

TULL
Enough.

Silence. Wendell strains. The coach shifts an inch, and everyone freezes. He looks up at Tull, eyes wide.

WENDELL
(whispering)
It's moving.

TULL
Keep going.

CORDELIA
(to herself)
Easy, easy.

The wheel pops free with a crack. The coach lurches forward. Wendell leaps aside. Cordelia lifts the rifle halfway. Tull cocks the shotgun, and for a long second the two of them regard each other down the length of the bridge.

TULL (CONT'D)
Let it pass.

CORDELIA
Why?

TULL
Because there's a preacher in the road.

She looks at Mbeki, still on his knees, and lowers the rifle.
